<header><p class="st-kicker">khanate · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Khoshut Khanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1642 CE – 1717 CE · capital Lhasa</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Oirat Mongol khanate that conquered Tibet for the Fifth Dalai Lama in 1642 and whose khans held the title King of Tibet until the Dzungar invasion of 1717.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a>, a chief of the Khoshut, one of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/four-oirat">Four Oirat</a>, answered an appeal from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a> leaders threatened by their rivals. He moved to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> region, defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khalkha-mongols">Khalkha</a> prince <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsogt-taij">Choghtu Khong Tayiji</a> in 1637, subdued the king of Beri in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kham">Kham</a> in 1640 and overthrew the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tsangpa-dynasty">Tsangpa dynasty</a> in 1642.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gushi-khan">Güshi Khan</a> took the title King of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/tibet-1912-1951">Tibet</a> but handed civil and religious authority to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/5th-dalai-lama">Fifth Dalai Lama</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ganden-phodrang">Ganden Phodrang</a>, keeping military power and protecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelug">Gelug</a>. Khoshut nomads settled around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a>, while the khans spent part of the year near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhasa">Lhasa</a>. The relationship produced a joint government in which the khan's real influence varied with each generation.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lhazang-khan">Lhazang Khan</a> killed the regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sangye-gyatso">Sangye Gyatso</a> in 1705 and deposed the Sixth <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalai-lama">Dalai Lama</a>, making many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibetans">Tibetans</a> hostile. In 1717 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/dzungar-khanate">Dzungar Khanate</a> invaded and killed him. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a> expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dzungars">Dzungars</a> in 1720 and crushed a Khoshut revolt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qinghai-lake">Kokonor</a> in 1723 to 1724.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/khoshut-khanate.jpg" alt="Khoshut Khanate" width="320" height="224"><figcaption>Image: Aizwal · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Standard_of_Gushri_khan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1642 CE – 1717 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>khanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>nomadic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lhasa</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Güshi Khan, Lhazang Khan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Tibetan Buddhism (Gelug)</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Oirat Mongolian, Tibetan</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Khoshut, Qoshot Khanate</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=khoshut-khanate" aria-label="Khoshut Khanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7052 1095 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Khoshut Khanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7372 1351L6951 879M7372 1351L7372 1351"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6951 879h0M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7372 1351h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7372 1351h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Lhasa. <a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=khoshut-khanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
